# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/010_integer_division_modulo.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 010_integer_division_modulo
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Integer division truncating toward zero and a dividend-signed remainder, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Integer division has exactly two undefined cases and both are provably absent from this
program.  No divisor is ever zero: every divisor is a nonzero literal or a volatile object
initialised to 2, -2 or 3 -- b_pos, b_neg, three, u_two and l_two -- and each initialiser is
visible in the source.  The most negative value divided by -1, which overflows and is
therefore undefined, is deliberately never formed: the large-magnitude case uses
-2147483647, which is one greater than the most negative int, and divides it by 3 rather
than by -1.
No signed overflow occurs anywhere else either.  Every quotient is representable in its own
type, and the widest product any identity check forms is 6 in magnitude, so no
multiplication can overflow.  No shift operator appears anywhere in the program.  There is
no aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the
relative addresses of unrelated objects.
Nothing depends on unspecified evaluation order.  Each volatile object is written exactly
once, by its own initialiser in its own declaration, so no modification is unsequenced
relative to any other access and no object is modified twice between sequence points.  The
volatile reads inside a single call are unsequenced relative to one another, but every read
of a given object yields the value its initialiser stored, because no volatile object is
ever written again; no printed value can therefore depend on the order in which a call's
arguments are evaluated, which is the property the one-side-effecting-argument rule exists
to secure -- obtained here by making the objects write-once rather than by counting them.
Truncation toward zero and a remainder taking the sign of the dividend are not
implementation-defined choices this program rests on: C11 6.5.5p6 makes / yield the
algebraic quotient with any fractional part discarded and requires (a / b) * b + a % b to
equal a wherever the quotient is representable, from which the remainder's sign follows.
The two identity lines assert that law directly, and both operands of every comparison in
them share a signedness, so no implicit signed-to-unsigned comparison is formed and the
full default warning gate passes with no deviation at all.
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer checks division by zero and the most negative value divided by
-1 specifically, and reports nothing for this program at -O0, -O1 or -O2, which is measured
evidence rather than an assertion that both undefined cases are absent.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This program takes NO target restriction, and that is an empirical conclusion rather than an
assumption.  Truncation toward zero and a remainder that takes the sign of the dividend were
measured to behave identically on all four supported targets, x86-64, i686, AArch64 and
RISC-V 64: -7 / 2 yields -3 and -7 % 2 yields -1 on every one of them, and all twelve
target-and-optimization configurations printed the same 319 bytes.  Because the four
backends were observed to agree, any cross-backend divergence in this program is a genuine
finding to report rather than an implementation-defined difference to mark, and oracle (b)
is therefore enabled for it.
The lowering differs sharply per target even though the results agree, which is what makes
the runtime variant valuable.  Measured at -O2: x86-64 and i686 divide with idivl, a single
instruction that yields the quotient and the remainder at once; AArch64 has sdiv but no
remainder instruction, so every remainder is synthesised with a following msub; and RISC-V
64 uses separate divw and remw instructions.  The 64-bit signed case becomes a helper call
on i686 -- __divdi3 and __moddi3 -- because that target has no 64-bit divide instruction at
all, and static linkage is what resolves those helpers with no dynamic loader involved.
long and unsigned long are deliberately never used.  Their width was measured at 4 bytes on
i686 and 8 bytes on the other three targets, so dividing a long would print two different
values across backends for reasons that have nothing to do with the compiler.  Only int and
unsigned int, 32 bits on all four targets, and long long, 64 bits on all four, appear here.
No object of plain char type is declared and no plain-char value is printed, because plain
char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V
64.  The only char in the program is the const char * parameter of the hand-declared printf
prototype, where no signedness is observable.  No pointer value is printed and no header is
included, so every bound this program relies on is spelled literally in its own source.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_pos_pos=3 1
fold_neg_pos=-3 -1
fold_pos_neg=-3 1
fold_neg_neg=3 -1
fold_exact=-4 0
fold_large=-715827882 -1
fold_unsigned=3 1
fold_llong=-3 -1
fold_identity=1
run_pos_pos=3 1
run_neg_pos=-3 -1
run_pos_neg=-3 1
run_neg_neg=3 -1
run_exact=-4 0
run_large=-715827882 -1
run_unsigned=3 1
run_llong=-3 -1
run_identity=1
END
